42
      "description": "BLOCKER THE BRIEF DID NOT ANTICIPATE — .claude/hooks/ IS GITIGNORED. The brief directs the hook to `.claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh`, 'same home as the existing .claude/hooks/git-commit-check.sh'. But .gitignore lines 16-19 read `.claude/*` with only `!.claude/agents/` and `!.claude/commands/` un-ignored, so .claude/hooks/ is ignored wholesale and does not exist in a linked worktree or a fresh clone at all (the three hooks in the primary checkout are untracked local files). A file written there is INVISIBLE TO GIT: it cannot be committed without -f, cannot be cherry-picked to mainline, would not appear in the reviewer's diff, and would be absent in CI — where the newly-registered test suite would then hard-fail looking for it. The slice would have delivered nothing.",
43
      "resolution": "Added the SMALLEST possible negation to .gitignore, immediately after the existing `!.claude/commands/` line, un-ignoring EXACTLY ONE file: `!.claude/hooks/` then `.claude/hooks/*` then `!.claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh`. Verified with `git add .claude/hooks/` that the ONLY staged path is .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh — git-commit-check.sh, session-start.sh and session-stop.sh stay ignored and untracked exactly as before. This matches the stated intent of the ignore block's own comment ('.claude/ holds SHARED harness tooling (tracked) alongside per-user/local files (ignored)'): the guard is shared, reviewed, CI-run tooling; the other three are per-user. .gitignore is not on the DO-NOT-TOUCH list, and a .gitignore change in a linked worktree has ZERO effect on any other worktree or on the live orchestrator until it is cherry-picked. FLAGGED FOR THE REVIEWER/ORCHESTRATOR: if you prefer, the alternative is to move the hook under the tracked scripts/ tree (e.g. scripts/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh) and drop the .gitignore hunk — the hook itself is path-agnostic and only the test's HOOK= line and the future settings.json wiring would move. I did NOT take that route because it deviates from the path the brief names."
