  "additional_changes_made_in_scope": [
52
    "FIX-2 SCOPE DISCIPLINE. Exactly the three permitted files were touched across the two fix-2 commits: .claude/hooks/seat-path-guard.sh, scripts/tests/test_seat_path_guard.sh, and this output.json. `git status --short` before the GREEN commit listed the single modified hook and nothing else. THE HOOK REMAINS UNWIRED: .claude/settings.json is untouched and still untracked, and suite arm 16 still resolves and greps the primary checkout's real settings.json and settings.local.json. Nothing else on the DO-NOT-TOUCH list moved: .claude/agents/*.md and every `tools:` frontmatter line, both ledgers and the .seq, the 0.8.20 plan/board/release-state, src/conformance/governed-surface-allowlist.json, dev/design/orchestration.md, AGENTS.md, and the already-reviewed .gitignore and scripts/agent-test.sh hunks were all left alone. Commits are additive on the existing branch; no landed commit was rewritten, no worktree was created/moved/removed, nothing was pushed.",
53
    "FIX-2 RED-FIRST EVIDENCE (TC-37 — an arm that has never been red proves nothing). The new arms were written and committed FIRST (824820df) and run against the unfixed fix-1 hook at 06b7418f: rc=1, 102 PASS / 11 FAIL. EVERY ONE OF THE 11 FAILURES WAS IN THE FALSE-NEGATIVE DIRECTION — 'expected permissionDecision=deny, got <silent>' with empty stdout, i.e. the guard allowing a src/** or engine/** write — which is the direction round 2 called the serious one. Verbatim list: arm 57 `sed -i -f - src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 57b `sed -i --file - src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 57c `perl -i -pe - src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 57d `sed -i - src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 62 `printf x > -/src/lib.rs`; arm 63 `git -C /home/x/wt checkout -- src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 63b `git -C /home/x/wt restore src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 63c `git -c core.editor=true checkout -- src/rust/lib.rs`; arm 64 `cp -t src/rust dev/plans/x.md`; arm 64b `cp --target-directory=src/rust dev/plans/x.md`; arm 64c `install -m 644 -t engine/native dev/plans/x.md`. After the fix: 113 PASS / 0 FAIL, rc=0, with all 56 pre-existing arms and all 102 previously-green assertions still green. The arms that were ALREADY GREEN when written (58, 58b, 59, 59b, 59c, 60, 60b, 60c, 61a-61g, 63d, 63e, 63f, 64d, 64e) SAY SO IN THE ARM TEXT rather than being presented as red-then-fixed; they are the regression pins for the paired true/false positives and for the header claims finding 2 was about.",
54
    "EVASION PATHS THE BASH DETECTION DOES NOT COVER — recorded here because the brief asked for them explicitly, and stated just as prominently in the script header under 'LIMITATIONS — READ BEFORE TRUSTING'. Bash guarding is BEST-EFFORT, NOT A SANDBOX; it raises the cost of an ACCIDENTAL violation and does not stop a determined one. Known unclosed evasions: (1) variable indirection — `D=src; printf x > \"$D/lib.rs\"`; (2) decoded or constructed commands — `base64 -d <<<... | sh`, `eval \"$(...)\"`; (3) command substitution in the target — `printf x > \"$(printf src/lib.rs)\"`; (4) writing through a helper — ./my-writer.sh, make, a cargo build script, any program the hook sees only by name; (5) `git apply` / `patch` — the PATCH BODY is never inspected, so a diff touching src/** passes unless a protected path appears in argv; (6) quoted paths containing spaces are tokenized naively (`read -ra` on whitespace), so `> \"src/my file.rs\"` yields the token `\"src/my` which still normalizes to a `src` segment and denies, but the reverse case — an allowed path containing a space — could mis-split; (7) `;` `|` `&&` INSIDE a quoted string split the command incorrectly (the splitter is textual, not a shell parser), which can both over- and under-block; (8) MultiEdit and NotebookEdit are NOT covered — only Edit, Write and Bash, per the brief's explicit three-tool scope. (8) is a named Phase-2 gap rather than a silent scope change; MultiEdit in particular carries file_path and would be a one-line addition if the reviewer wants it now.",
55
    "ACCEPTED OVER-BLOCK, documented in the header: because segment matching runs over the WHOLE normalized path, a path whose ancestor directory is literally named src/engine/tests OUTSIDE this repo (the common `~/src/notes.md` shape) is denied for a guarded seat. Deliberate: guarded seats work inside the repo, over-blocking is the safe direction for a guard whose purpose is stopping source writes, and the deny reason names the path so a human can see instantly what happened. The alternative (anchoring to $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR) would have broken the brief's requirement that the guard work for paths inside ANY worktree.",
56
    "NOTE ON `engine/**`, following the observation ASH-A already recorded in its own output.json: `engine/**` is a CONCEPTUAL path — the engine crate physically lives at src/rust/crates/fathomdb-engine and is therefore already covered by the `src` segment rule. The hook implements a literal `engine` segment anyway, so both the conceptual and any future real engine/ tree are covered. Arms 10, 19 and 21 use literal engine/ paths.",
